export const metaDescription = "Thermal vacuum testing explained: TVAC cycling, thermal balance, bakeout, standards and chamber selection. Jetsys Defence designs and supports space simulation systems.";
export const title = "Thermal Vacuum Testing";

export const introduction = [
  "Hardware that runs cool on a bench can overheat within hours in orbit. There is no air to carry heat away, so the convective cooling every terrestrial design quietly depends on simply stops. The same hardware swings through large temperature extremes every orbit, while adhesives and cable insulation release volatiles that condense on optics and detectors. None of this is reliably predictable from analysis alone — which is why flight hardware goes into a chamber that reproduces both conditions at once before it flies.",
  "Thermal vacuum testing exposes hardware to high vacuum and controlled temperature extremes at the same time, to verify that it survives and performs in the environment it will operate in. The term covers a family of tests rather than one procedure — cycling, thermal balance, bakeout and materials screening — and they are separated below, because the distinction decides what a campaign costs and what it actually proves.",
  "It is also not only a satellite subject. High-altitude avionics, UAV and HAPS payloads, sensor assemblies and vacuum-exposed instruments are qualified the same way. This page is written for satellite and payload developers, space startups, defence research establishments, electronics manufacturers and test facility planners. Jetsys Defence designs, builds, integrates and supports thermal vacuum chambers and test systems from Navi Mumbai.",
];

